{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 13, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Summary of the Wikipedia Page \"Raymond III Count of Tripoli\"**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age. Raymond III was a key figure in the Crusader states during the 12th century.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. He was involved in the succession disputes of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. In 1174 he supported Miles of Plancy as regent for Baldwin IV of Jerusalem. After Miles was assassinated he helped establish Raymond of Tripoli as regent in 1177. Raymond of Tripoli was his cousin and they worked together to stabilize the kingdom. Raymond III was also involved in the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 where he fought alongside Baldwin IV against Saladin. He was a vocal opponent of Guy of Lusignan who became king in 1186.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\nRaymond III is perhaps best known for his role in the lead up to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the leaders who advised against engaging Saladin’s forces directly. However his advice was ignored and the Crusader army was crushed at Hattin. Raymond III managed to escape the battle and fled to Tripoli. He died shortly after in 1187 without a male heir. His death marked the end of the original Tripoli dynasty.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\nRaymond III’s death had significant consequences for the Crusader states. Without an heir Tripoli passed to his cousin Bohemond III of Antioch. This transfer weakened the defenses of Tripoli and left it vulnerable. The fall of Tripoli to Saladin in 1187 was partly due to the instability following Raymond III’s death. His legacy is one of both political maneuvering and military misfortune. Raymond III remains an important figure in the history of the Crusades and the Crusader states.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.17142000794410706}, {"token": "Summary", "logprob": -1.2848113775253296}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00039426659350283444}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.1638595461845398}, {"token": " Wikipedia", "logprob": -7.10462118149735e-05}, {"token": " Page", "logprob": -0.8981921672821045}, {"token": " \"", "logprob": -0.5842089653015137}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -6.437094270950183e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.07287787614041e-05}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -4.756337511935271e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.005982705857604742}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.005352093372494e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "\"", "logprob": -0.0011261318577453494}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -3.8742269680369645e-05}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.05218442901968956}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -1.4066597032069694e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.2980702519416809}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.13514982163906097}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -4.6491513785440475e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0005585778853856027}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0006982034537941217}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.1324817933200393e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -9.154854342341423e-05}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.0006255338666960597}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -9.047575440490618e-05}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.38697415590286255}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.011089242994785309}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.4066597032069694e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.936446541454643e-05}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -3.0517112463712692e-05}, {"token": "7", "logprob": -3.1709168979432434e-05}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.022763842716813087},